    <body>
      <sec id="article-30675.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Tufted hair folliculitis is an uncommon progressive disorder associated with cicatricial (scarring) alopecia of the scalp and can present a diagnostic challenge. A key feature is the presence of multiple hairs (often more than 6) emerging from a single follicular opening, a finding commonly linked to folliculitis decalvans but not exclusive to it. This activity emphasizes that tufted hairs reflect a late-stage scarring process in which dermal fibrosis causes follicular ostia to converge. Learners review the pathophysiology, clinical presentation, and trichoscopic features of this condition, as well as its overlap with other dermatologic disorders. The course underscores the importance of clinical context and a broad differential diagnosis. Also outlined are current evaluation and management strategies while highlighting the role of the interprofessional team in improving diagnostic accuracy and patient outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify key clinical, dermoscopic, and histologic signs associated with tufted hair folliculitis and related scarring alopecias.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Determine appropriate diagnostic evaluations, including biopsy and trichoscopy, to identify underlying causes and contributing inflammatory or infectious processes.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Evaluate available treatment options, including medical and supportive therapies, to minimize harm and optimize patient outcomes in the absence of definitive therapy.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Coordinate interprofessional team strategies for improving care and communication to advance the treatment of tufted hair folliculitis and improve outcomes.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=30675&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=30675">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30675.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Tufted hair folliculitis is an uncommon clinicopathologic feature associated with cicatricial alopecia. This condition is characterized by clusters of 5 to 30 hair shafts emerging from a single dilated follicular opening, often resembling a doll&#x02019;s hair pattern. Also known as pili multigemini, tufted hairs were first described by Flemming in 1883 and later by Giovannini in 1892. This finding is characteristic of folliculitis decalvans but may also be observed in other scarring alopecias and hair disorders. Although tufted hairs are not independently diagnostic, recognition is important for evaluating underlying disease, guiding management, and informing interprofessional care.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r1">[1]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30675.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The etiology of tufted hair folliculitis remains poorly defined. Rather than representing a distinct disease entity, it is a clinicopathologic feature observed in association with multiple forms of cicatricial alopecia. This finding likely reflects a late stage of follicular injury, in which dermal fibrosis results in the convergence of adjacent follicular units.</p>
        <p>Tufted hairs have been reported in a variety of conditions, including scars from trauma, burns, or surgery; acne keloidalis nuchae; folliculitis decalvans; dissecting cellulitis of the scalp; tinea capitis; lichen planus; and pemphigus vulgaris. Cases have also been associated with medications such as cyclosporine, trastuzumab, and lapatinib. Some authors have proposed that tufted hair folliculitis arises from preexisting folliculitis, whereas others have considered it a variant of folliculitis decalvans, given the overlapping histopathologic features.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30675.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Tufted hair folliculitis most commonly occurs in adults during the fourth and fifth decades of life, although cases have been reported in patients ranging from 10 to 68 years of age. Cases in younger patients are uncommon, including a few associated with Gobello nevus syndrome. There is no established racial predilection, but a male predominance has been observed, with a male-to-female ratio of approximately 2.7:1.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30675.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Numerous pathogenic mechanisms for tufted hair folliculitis have been suggested, including nevoid anomalies, recurrent follicular infections, and retention of telogen hairs within the tufts. Tufted hair folliculitis results from fibrosis, causing the convergence of adjacent follicular units, along with retention of telogen hairs across multiple hair cycles. Some authors hypothesize that tufted hair folliculitis may arise from nevoid scalp structures undergoing inflammation and scarring.</p>
        <p>Staphylococcal colonies are frequently cultured from lesions, but their role in pathogenesis remains unclear. Other authors have suggested a local immune response disorder may contribute, although no host immune abnormalities have been demonstrated, with CD4 and CD8 counts and the T-cell/B-cell ratio remaining within normal ranges.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;There is a proposal that altered host immunity may involve the interleukin 1&#x003b2; signaling pathway and the TH17 immune response.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r6">[6]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30675.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>Under light microscopy, a typical outer root sheath encasing all hair shafts can be observed; the follicular groups generally contain five or more hair shafts and correspond histopathologically to compound follicular units joined by their outer root sheaths. Histopathologic analysis of tufted hairs usually demonstrates scarring fibrosis within the upper dermis, leading to a convergence of the follicular ostia and the emergence of multiple hairs from a single follicular orifice. The fibrosis process, therefore, remains isolated and does not affect the lower regions of the hair follicle, including the bulb.</p>
        <p>In folliculitis decalvans, the scarring process is primarily driven by a neutrophilic inflammatory infiltrate (see <bold>Image</bold>. Folliculitis Decalvans, H&#x00026;E X1000). In other disorders, the inflammatory infiltrate may be more heterogeneous, including neutrophils, lymphocytes, and plasma cells.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Degeneration of sebaceous glands and interfollicular acanthosis are noted, especially in the initial phases. Chronic cases may show a lymphohistiocytic infiltrate accompanied by a foreign-body reaction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r7">[7]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30675.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Patients with tufted hair folliculitis generally present with scalp pain, itching, and swelling, along with slowly progressive hair loss. Perifollicular crusting and scaling are also frequently observed. The process usually begins as a localized lesion on the scalp that gradually expands.</p>
        <p>The hallmark clinical finding is multiple anagen hairs emerging from a single dilated follicular opening, resembling doll&#x02019;s hair. These tufts are interspersed with circumscribed areas of scarring alopecia lacking visible follicular openings. The parietal and occipital regions are most commonly involved, although the entire scalp may be affected.</p>
        <p>The beard is rarely involved; it may be possible to express purulent exudate from the dilated follicular openings, though pustules are not present. Clinical features may vary with disease duration and underlying etiology, with findings including erythema, edema, tenderness, and scalp bogginess. A linear distribution has been reported in a case of tufted hair folliculitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r8">[8]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30675.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Trichoscopic examination of affected areas demonstrates multiple hair shafts emerging from a single follicular opening. Hair follicles are encircled by perifollicular scaling and white starburst-like structures, indicative of fibrosis. Blood vessels may sometimes be observed between the hair follicles.</p>
        <p>Other characteristic findings may include yellowish tubular scaling with distal widening, follicular pustules, crusts, and concentric vascular patterns around emerging hair shafts. Perifollicular erythema and white scaling have been observed in the folliculitis decalvans&#x02013;lichen planopilaris phenotypic spectrum. In advanced disease, trichoscopy shows white and milky-red areas with absent follicular openings, reflecting permanent follicular destruction.</p>
        <p>Trichoscopy-guided scalp biopsy with histopathologic analysis is typically recommended to confirm the diagnosis of tufted hair folliculitis. Biopsy specimens should be taken from the margin of alopecic lesions where trichoscopic signs of disease activity, such as tufted hairs, perifollicular scaling, and follicular pustules, are present. Biopsy specimens taken from the central portion of the alopecic area typically show fibrotic tissue, which may obscure the underlying etiology.</p>
        <p>In cases of active primary scarring alopecia, such as lichen planus, characteristic histopathologic features will be evident. A scalp biopsy can also indicate the extent of scarring and the degree of active inflammation, providing prognostic information about the condition. Bacterial cultures often yield <italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus&#x000a0;</italic>species, although this organism is not seen in all cases, and treatment of&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus</italic>&#x000a0;does not consistently lead to disease resolution. Fungal cultures typically are negative.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r9">[9]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30675.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>There is no standardized treatment for tufted hair folliculitis, and no therapy has proven to be consistently effective. If a cause can be identified, treatment is directed to the underlying disease. Otherwise, therapy should focus on symptom control and aesthetic improvement.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Oral antibiotics are&#x000a0;recommended for patients with folliculitis decalvans and moderate-to-severe inflammation. In individuals with severe disease activity, a brief regimen of oral glucocorticosteroids may&#x000a0;be advantageous. Oral isotretinoin&#x000a0;is considered&#x000a0;the primary treatment for patients with mild active disease characterized by perifollicular erythema and hyperkeratosis in the absence of pustules or crusts. This medication is also recommended for resistant cases or persistent inflammatory lesions.&#x000a0;Topical or intralesional corticosteroids should be considered as adjuncts to systemic treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>Topical treatments such as shampoos containing salicylic acid or&#x000a0;tar derivatives can reduce scaling but are generally insufficient as monotherapy. Topical steroids can reduce erythema, edema, and itching, but their effects are minimal and temporary.&#x000a0;In one instance, the combination of mometasone propionate and fusidic acid cream demonstrated sustained efficacy at 2-year follow-up.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>A 42-year-old man experienced symptom remission and partial hair regrowth at 12 months after treatment with topical ciclosporin-A 1.5% applied twice daily for 1 month, followed by once daily for 3 months.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;Topical tacrolimus has also been employed, but symptoms recur upon discontinuation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r12">[12]</xref> Dapsone has demonstrated efficacy in treating conditions characterized by aberrant neutrophil infiltration. Topical formulations reduce the risk of hematologic adverse effects seen with oral administration.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r13">[13]</xref> One study's results showed that monotherapy with Manuka honey resulted in longer remission than any other topical or oral therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>Systemic antistaphylococcal agents may be used in tufted hair folliculitis when there are clear clinical signs of infection (eg, purulent exudate). Prolonged treatment with oral rifampicin (300 mg daily) plus oral clindamycin (300 mg daily) yielded good results. Excision of scarring alopecia plaques has been reported to achieve favorable cosmetic outcomes in patients with tufted hair folliculitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;Radiation therapy and surgical excision have been attempted, but results have been inconsistent.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;One instance of adipose tissue transplantation in active disease was documented, demonstrating improvements in inflammation, hair vitality, and partial hair regrowth; however, the duration of these effects remains uncertain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Treatment with the Nd:YAG laser led to improvement in approximately 75% of reported cases. Use of a long-pulsed alexandrite laser has been described in a single case, with sustained, significant improvement at 6-month follow-up. Additionally, the combination of CO2 laser therapy and photodynamic therapy in three patients demonstrated substantial improvement at 12-month follow-up.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Platelet-rich plasma therapy was associated with clinical improvement in 2 patients with refractory folliculitis decalvans, a condition for which few effective treatment options exist.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r21">[21]</xref> Botulinum toxin injection was associated with complete remission that persisted for 5 years of follow-up.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;In a 37-year-old man who showed no improvement with isotretinoin, rifampicin, or topical corticosteroids, disease control was achieved by maintaining his hair at a number 1 (3 mm) clipper length.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;Biologic agents, including adalimumab, Janus kinase inhibitors (preferably baricitinib and tofacitinib), and oral dapsone, hydroxychloroquine, and cyclosporine, have also been reported to be effective.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;As with other forms of scarring alopecia, hair transplantation may be considered for patients with folliculitis decalvans when disease activity is absent.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r24">[24]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30675.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Tufted hairs are commonly observed on adult beards and on the scalps of children, but their presence on the scalp in adults may indicate underlying pathology. Tufted hairs are a definitive diagnostic feature of folliculitis decalvans, although they may also appear in other hair disorders, alongside additional trichoscopic findings.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r25">[25]</xref> Conditions in which tufted hairs may be observed include acne keloidalis nuchae, dissecting cellulitis of the scalp, folliculitis decalvans, erosive pustular dermatosis of the scalp, lichen planopilaris, folliculitis decalvans&#x02013;lichen planopilaris phenotypic spectrum, central centrifugal cicatricial alopecia, and tinea capitis.</p>
        <p>Acne keloidalis nuchae is a chronic idiopathic inflammatory condition characterized by fibrotic plaques, papules, and hair loss affecting the occiput and nape. Early cases demonstrate&#x000a0;pustules with a white halo around hair follicles, indicating fibrosis. These findings are more readily observed when firm pressure is applied during dermoscopy. In advanced cases, tufted hairs and dilated follicular ostia can be seen. Recent research suggests that tufted hairs in acne keloidalis nuchae may reflect an overlap syndrome with folliculitis decalvans, rather than a late-stage feature of the condition. Approximately 20% of cases present with both conditions, particularly in Black individuals.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;See <bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Acne Keloidalis Nuchae, Dermoscopy.</p>
        <p>Dissecting cellulitis of the scalp is a chronic inflammatory condition with uncertain etiopathogenesis, characterized by boggy, suppurative, and painful nodules frequently accompanied by patchy hair loss. This may occur in conjunction with other follicular occlusive conditions such as acne conglobata, hidradenitis suppurativa, and pilonidal sinus. In the initial phases of the disease, broken hair shafts of varying lengths and black dots may be observed. Large yellow-brown dots, double-bordered and occasionally containing dystrophic shafts, contribute to the characteristic 3-dimensional, or soap bubble, appearance, which is the most distinctive trichoscopic feature of the condition. A disrupted yellow area with pustules signifies&#x000a0;pus lakes encircling the follicular openings. In advanced stages of the disease, tufted hairs (polytrichia), along with white dots and amorphous, structureless white areas resulting from fibrosis, may be observed. See <bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Dissecting Cellulitis of the Scalp.</p>
        <p>Folliculitis decalvans is a progressive neutrophil-associated scarring alopecia characterized by recurrent pustular follicular lesions. The primary characteristic is the presence of 10 or more hair shafts originating from a single follicular opening. Tufts of hair represent the most distinctive pathognomonic feature and may be accompanied by perifollicular erythema and hemorrhage, follicular pustules with scaling, and yellow crusts during the acute stage.</p>
        <p>Erosive pustular dermatosis of the scalp is marked by solitary or multiple alopecic patches&#x000a0;with sterile pustules, superficial erosions, serum discharge, and brownish-yellow crusts. These lesions can cause diffuse atrophy and scarring alopecia if untreated. Skin atrophy and visible hair bulbs are the most&#x000a0;notable&#x000a0;trichoscopic findings, along with the absence of follicular ostia, enlarged dermal blood vessels, and serous or gray-brown perifollicular hyperpigmentation. Additionally, pili torti, damaged hair shafts, black crusts, and thick yellow exudate have been reported. Starace et al found that tufted hairs are a common trichoscopic sign in 61.5% of patients with erosive pustular dermatosis of the scalp.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r30">[30]</xref></p>
        <p>Lichen planopilaris is an inflammatory condition of the scalp and is considered the most common cause of cicatricial alopecia. The most prevalent trichoscopic characteristics of classic lichen planopilaris&#x000a0;comprise the absence of follicular openings, perifollicular desquamation, white scar regions, and perifollicular erythema. Recent literature has described FDLPPPS (olliculitis decalvans, lichen planopilaris, pseudopelade of Brocq, primary scarring alopecia) as a condition characterized by features of folliculitis decalvans (follicular pustules and hair tufts), occurring either concurrently or sequentially with features of lichen planopilaris (perifollicular erythema and white perifollicular scaling). There has been a proposal that this biphasic clinical presentation may stem from lichenoid changes observed in the later, inactive phase of folliculitis decalvans.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>Central centrifugal cicatricial alopecia is a distinct kind of scarring alopecia characterized by permanent hair loss on the vertex or crown of the scalp with centrifugal spreading. Though ubiquitous, it is more prevalent in Black women. Trichoscopy typically demonstrates a peripilar gray-white halo, loss of follicular openings, disrupted honeycomb pigmentation, pinpoint white dots, variability in hair shaft diameter, white patches, interfollicular scaling, and broken hairs. In this type of alopecia, 2 to 3 hair shafts may emerge from a single follicular ostium.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r31">[31]</xref></p>
        <p>Tinea capitis is a&#x000a0;fungal scalp infection primarily caused by<italic toggle="yes"> Microsporum</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">Trichophyton</italic> species. The condition primarily affects children aged 3 to 7, but it can also affect adults, particularly older populations or those who are immunocompromised. This condition is characterized by trichoscopic signs such as comma, corkscrew, Morse code, zigzag, bent, block, and I-shaped hairs. Baroni et al noted that tinea capitis can imitate tufted folliculitis, presenting with scarring alopecia of the scalp, with scales and tufts of hair arising from single follicles.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r32">[32]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30675.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The progression of the disease is generally chronic and characterized by relapses.&#x000a0;Prolonged treatment beyond the remission of symptoms may be deemed necessary to prevent recurrences.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r10">[10]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30675.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Common complications of tufted hair folliculitis include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Scarring alopecia: This includes persistent inflammation that damages hair follicles, leading to their replacement with scar tissue. Once follicles are removed, hair cannot regenerate, resulting in persistent bald patches.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Chronic pain and discomfort: Patients often report continuous scalp tenderness, pruritus, and a sensation of bogginess or swelling in the affected regions.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Secondary bacterial infections:&#x000a0;Because&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus aureus</italic> is frequently present, it may lead to secondary pustules, purulent exudate, and crusting, exacerbating the condition.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Psychosocial impact:&#x000a0;Visible cosmetic disfigurement and persistent hair loss may result in considerable emotional distress, such as anxiety, depression, and diminished self-esteem.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Surgical complications:&#x000a0;In some instances, aggressive surgical procedures to treat the condition may cause additional damage, such as increased scarring or the spread of inflammation.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30675.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Since tufted hair folliculitis is associated with scarring alopecia, the objective of any treatment is to manage inflammation, prevent more hair loss, and alleviate symptoms. Hair regrowth cannot be anticipated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r33">[33]</xref>&#x000a0;Early consultation and follow-up are crucial to prevent further scarring and to identify the underlying conditions responsible for the hair tufting.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30675.s14" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Tufted hair folliculitis usually has a chronic progressive course with alternating periods of exacerbation and recovery. The condition is generally not life-threatening. However, it may have an adverse psychological effect due to the cosmetic disfigurement that it may cause.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Combination therapy, especially involving systemic antibiotics alongside topical, local, and other systemic agents, may yield the most favorable outcomes and the longest duration of effect for patients. A more prominent role for biologic agents and laser therapy in their management is also proposed, along with the use of biopsy and bacterial samples to improve diagnostic accuracy and guide treatment strategies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30675.r19">[19]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30675.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Many scalp conditions can manifest as tufted folliculitis. If a cause can be identified, treatment is aimed at the underlying disease. Otherwise, therapy should focus on reducing discomfort and improving appearance. To improve outcomes, clinicians should not aggressively pursue surgical options, as they often cause more harm than benefit.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Managing tufted hair folliculitis is best coordinated by an interprofessional team that includes dermatologists, surgeons, advanced practitioners, mental health nurses, and pharmacists.&#x000a0;A strategic approach is equally crucial, involving evidence-based strategies to optimize treatment plans and minimize&#x000a0;adverse effects. Ethical considerations must guide decision-making, ensuring informed consent and respecting patient autonomy in treatment choices.</p>
        <p>Each healthcare professional must be aware of their responsibilities and contribute their unique expertise to the patient's care plan, fostering a multidisciplinary approach. Effective interprofessional communication is paramount, allowing seamless information exchange and collaborative decision-making among the team members. Care coordination plays a pivotal role in ensuring that the patient's journey from diagnosis to treatment and follow-up is well-managed, minimizing errors and enhancing patient safety. By embracing these principles of skill, strategy, ethics, responsibilities, interprofessional communication, and care coordination, healthcare professionals can deliver patient-centered care, ultimately improving patient outcomes and enhancing team performance in the management of tufted hair folliculitis.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30675.s16">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
